The legal texts come from SierraLII (Sierra Leone Legal Information Institute, on the Laws.Africa Peachjam platform):

  SierraLII, https://sierralii.gov.sl, acts as published, read from archived copies of its
  pages on the Internet Archive (https://web.archive.org), retrieved 2026-10-28.

Licence and terms, as SierraLII states them: CC BY-NC 4.0, crediting SierraLII as the source (https://sierralii.gov.sl/terms-of-use/, s. 9).
CC BY-NC 4.0: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/legalcode

Attribution:

  Source: SierraLII (https://sierralii.gov.sl), licensed under the Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial 4.0 International licence (CC BY-NC 4.0).

The texts may be used only for non-commercial purposes. This repository is private; whether and how it
may be published is the owner's decision under these terms.

Changes made by Lexifina: each version was converted to one Markdown file per provision, one paragraph per
line; spacing, quotes and dashes were normalised; editorial notes, Part and Chapter headings were left out;
and the versions were arranged as git commits credited to the laws SierraLII names for each change.
The wording of the law was not changed. Neither SierraLII nor Laws.Africa endorses this repository.

Lexifina's own contributions (the arrangement of the history, the commit messages, the tables and the
tools) are dedicated to the public domain under CC0 1.0 Universal:
https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode

The Constitution of Sierra Leone, 1991 and its amending Acts (folder constitution/) come from the
Parliament of Sierra Leone, https://www.parliament.gov.sl, read from Internet Archive copies of its PDFs.
They are Acts of Parliament; parliament.gov.sl states no licence. The 1991 text and the 2001 amending Act
are Sierra Leone Web (https://www.sierra-leone.org) transcriptions published by Parliament. Lexifina
applied each amending Act's instructions to the text before it; the wording of each Act was not changed.
